    "waistToHeightMethodTitle": "Waist-to-Height Ratio",
    "@waistToHeightMethodTitle": {
        "description": "Method info title"
    },
    "waistToHeightMethodSummary": "Your waist divided by your height, both in centimeters, from the same day's waist measurement and your profile height. NICE uses it to screen for central fat: 0.40 to 0.49 is the healthy range (keep your waist to less than half your height), 0.50 to 0.59 is increased risk and 0.60 or more is high risk. The bands apply to all sexes and ethnicities, including people with a lot of muscle. This is screening, not a diagnosis.",
    "@waistToHeightMethodSummary": {
        "description": "Method info for waistToHeightRatio: its summary"
    },
    "waistToHeightMethodLimitations": "NICE gives these bands for people with a BMI under 35. Whether NICE sets a band below 0.40 could not be confirmed, so Compound shows none. A tape measurement can vary by a centimeter or two with where and how it is taken.",
    "@waistToHeightMethodLimitations": {
        "description": "Method info for waistToHeightRatio: its limitations"
    },
    "waistToHipMethodTitle": "Waist-to-Hip Ratio",
    "@waistToHipMethodTitle": {
        "description": "Method info title"
    },
    "waistToHipMethodSummary": "Your waist divided by your hips, from measurements logged on the same day. A WHO expert consultation marks a substantially increased risk of metabolic complications from 0.90 in men and 0.85 in women. Compound uses the cut-off for the sex in your profile, and shows both when it is not set. This is screening, not a diagnosis.",
    "@waistToHipMethodSummary": {
        "description": "Method info for waistToHipRatio: its summary"
    },
    "waistToHipMethodLimitations": "The cut-offs come from population studies and were not set for any one ethnicity or for very muscular people. Waist-to-height is the better-supported ratio for lifters.",
    "@waistToHipMethodLimitations": {
        "description": "Method info for waistToHipRatio: its limitations"
    },
    "goalProgressMethodSummary": "Progress is how far your trend weight, the smoothed line on the Weight Trend screen, has moved from where the goal started toward its target, from 0% to 100%. Using the trend rather than your last weigh-in means a single heavy or light day barely moves it. Moving away from the target reads as 0%, not negative.",
    "@goalProgressMethodSummary": {
        "description": "Method info for goalProgress: its summary"
    },
    "goalProgressMethodLimitations": "Day-to-day weight varies by about half a percent of body weight from water and food alone, so the trend lags a real change by a week or two.",
    "@goalProgressMethodLimitations": {
        "description": "Method info for goalProgress: its limitations"
    },
